  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potent storm system moves across PA tonight and\nFri with a severe threat Fri aftn/eve. \n\nA surface low moving through the Midwest will cross the Great\nLakes tonight and Friday. Showers and possibly a thunderstorm \nor two will develop tonight ahead of a warm front as it lifts \nnorthward into the region. Most guidance shows a break in the \nshowers for much of the CWA during the late morning/early \nafternoon which will allow for at least some air mass \ndestabilization before a strong cold front begins to approach \nfrom the west. The HREF shows surface CAPE approaching 1000 J/kg\nfor areas east of I-99 which will be more than sufficient for \nwidespread thunderstorm development along the front. With 0-6 km\nbulk shear expected to be in the 40 to 60 knot range and \noriented nearly parallel to the surface boundary, initial storms\nshould quickly merge into a low-topped squall line capable of \nproducing strong to severe wind gusts. SPC has a wind driven \nslight risk of severe weather over the southeastern half of the \narea to highlight this threat.\n\nWe will also need to watch the leading edge of the line for\nbrief QLCS tornadoes as model soundings show between 20 and 30\nknots of 0-1 km shear and over 100 J/kg of 0-3 km CAPE that may\naid in stretching any vorticity. The threat will likely be\nmaximized (although a much lower chance than the potential for \ndamaging wind gusts) during the afternoon over southeast PA, and\nthis is where SPC has included a 2 percent tornado contour.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Windy with first flakes possible on the higher \nterrain of the north Fri night/Sat morning\n\nThe post-frontal pressure gradient is pretty tight with 10mb of\ndrop across the state Friday night and Saturday morning. 50+KT \nwinds behind the front look like they will stay AOA 6-7kft and \nsoundings from both the RAP and the NAM show winds at the top of\nthe mixed layer remaining below 40 knots. This should prevent \nwind gusts from getting any stronger than 35 or 40 mph and a \nWind Advisory appears much less likely than it did a few days \nago.\n\n850 mb temps over Lake Erie will fall to around -5C by Saturday\nmorning as a much colder air mass moves in. This is certainly\ncold enough to support lake effect clouds with lake surface \ntemps still in the low 60s, but the potential for widespread \nrain or snow showers continues to decrease. Northerly flow will \nresult in a very short fetch across the lakes, so moisture will \nbe limited. Even though model RH profiles do show saturation in\nthe DGZ, significantly drier air below that level should prevent\nmuch of the precipitation from reaching the ground. That being\nsaid, most models do still show a few light rain or snow showers\ndeveloping over the northern tier. The window of opportunity for\nthese showers will be rather short as drier air moves in by\nmid-morning.\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Cold weather may end the growing season for many\nplaces Sun morning. \n\nA large surface high moves in for the weekend, supplying dry \nbut cold weather for Saturday and Sunday. Saturday will hold \nthe coldest daytime temps with temps getting just above 40F in \nthe NE and 52F in the SE. Those maxes will be 20-30F lower than\nFriday's maxes. \n\nThe current forecast show temperature dropping below freezing\nSaturday morning over parts of northwest PA, but there is still\nsome uncertainty regarding just how low temperatures get since\nit should stay pretty cloudy for much of the night. If\nconfidence continues to increase in parts of the region dipping\nbelow 32, a Freeze Warning may be needed for some zones across\nnorth-central PA. McKean County would be left out of any freeze\nproducts since their growing season has already ended. Frost is\nless of a concern Friday night/Saturday morning due to the\naforementioned cloud cover as well as the fact that winds \nlikely stay pretty gusty.\n\nSunday morning will be the most-likely time for sub-freezing \ntemps across much of the CWA. This could end the growing season\nfor a significant portion of the forecast area. A widespread \nfrost is almost a certainty as winds decrease and go mainly calm\nSun AM. We'll be considering appropriate frost/freeze products,\nbut it is still too early to be that confident on details.\nSunday night will likely also feature fairly widespread frost,\nbut the location of any frost/freeze headlines will depend on \nwhere the growing season remains active after the previous\nnight's freeze.\n\nHigh pressure is expected to remain in control for at least the\nfirst half of next week. This will result in mainly dry weather\nwith high temperatures returning to the upper 50s and 60s.",
